{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction Law criminal-conviction and civil-liability grounds","requirement":"Landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or civilly found liable for, assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, family or employees, theft, drug activity or human trafficking, or who knowingly permits such a person to reside there. Three-day Notice to Quit; two-year filing limit.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; 2-year filing limit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of residential dwelling units covered by the New Jersey eviction law; also applies where the tenant knowingly permits an offender to reside at the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"The civil-liability ground does not apply to a tenant allowing a juvenile to reside there where the juvenile was found delinquent due to drug use or possession.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwelling eviction causes and notice","requirement":"A tenant of a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be evicted only by court order, for nonpayment, holding over, disorderly conduct, willful damage or constant violation of written rules or lease terms. Notice: three months (at-will/year-to-year), one month (month-to-month), three days (cause).","key_value":"3-month / 1-month / 3-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction causes and notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, subject to statutory exceptions.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations","requirement":"A landlord who evicts a tenant to occupy the unit must occupy it six months; otherwise the landlord owes the tenant three times damages plus fees. After a demolition or board-up eviction, no residential use for five years; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation.","key_value":"3x damages + fees; $10,000 civil penalty; 5-year use bar","coverage_conditions":"Evictions carried out on the grounds of landlord personal occupancy or permanent removal of the building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"evictions based on owner occupancy or demolition/board-up"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000.00 for each violation of the permanent-removal provision.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"If the landlord allows any residential use of the unit during the five (5) year period from the date the unit became vacant, the landlord, or the former landlord, may be liable to the tenant for three (3) times the damages plus attorney fees and costs.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection for tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, alter the lease or refuse to renew in reprisal for a tenant enforcing lease or legal rights, complaining to a government authority about health or safety violations, joining a tenant organization, or refusing a lease change made in response.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"excludes owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction notice requirements","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction. Where a buyer wants to occupy the foreclosed property as a primary residence, the landlord must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit, and a lease running beyond 90 days is honored until it expires.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental tenants in property purchased at foreclosure, including month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units, which are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental property sold in foreclosure"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is no more than three times the county average per-person income or $50,000.","key_value":"40 years; income limit 3x county average per-person income or $50,000","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership who lived there at least one year before the conversion recording date and meet the age, disability or veterans criteria.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"condominium, cooperative or fee simple conversions; one year of residency before conversion recording"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 conversion protections","requirement":"Extends protected-tenancy eviction protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties (currently only Hudson County) in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, in buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Hudson County only (per N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.2(b))"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on rent increases above rent control ordinances","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent in excess of any applicable rent control or rent leveling ordinance; where the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase is prohibited.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":null,"applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
